    "summary": "The contest for Speaker of the House of the People has narrowed to two candidates: Deputy Minister of Interior Sadaad Caliyow and Minister of Ports Cabdulqaadir Maxamed Nuur Jaamac. Former Foreign Minister Cabdisalaan Dhay has withdrawn from the race.",
    "content": "The election for the Speaker of the House of the People of Somalia has entered a new phase, with two candidates remaining in contention. The final contenders are Deputy Minister of Interior Sadaad Caliyow and Minister of Ports Cabdulqaadir Maxamed Nuur Jaamac. Former Foreign Minister Cabdisalaan Dhay has reportedly withdrawn from the contest, leaving the field clear for the two remaining aspirants.\n\nBoth candidates are considered influential political figures with strong ties to Villa Somalia, the seat of the Somali presidency. Their connections to the executive branch have made the race highly competitive, as each commands significant political backing. The election is being closely observed by political analysts and the public alike.\n\nThe contest for the speakership comes at a time when Somalia's federal parliament is navigating key legislative agendas, including constitutional reforms and security sector development. The outcome of this election could shape the balance of power between the executive and legislative branches.\n\nFor the Somali diaspora, the election of the lower house speaker holds particular significance. The speaker's role influences parliamentary oversight of diaspora engagement policies, including voting rights for Somalis abroad and investment from diaspora communities. A speaker with close ties to the executive may expedite or delay diaspora-related initiatives.\n\nThe election is expected to take place in the coming weeks, as outlined by parliamentary procedures. Both candidates are currently conducting outreach to secure the required votes from fellow MPs.\n\nRead the original report from Goobjoog here: https://goobjoog.com/2026/07/26/laba-musharax-oo-kusoo-haray-tartanka-loogu-jiro-guddoomiyaha-golaha-shacabka/",
