    "content": "The Puntland Ministry of Interior, Federal and Democratization Affairs released a statement urging an immediate cessation of hostilities between warring clans in the Sanaag region. The appeal comes after recent clashes that resulted in casualties and displacement of civilians. The ministry emphasized the need for dialogue and reconciliation to restore peace.\n\nClan conflicts in Sanaag have historically been triggered by disputes over land, water resources, and political representation. The region, which is contested between Puntland and Somaliland, has seen periodic violence that undermines local governance and development efforts. Elders and civil society groups have previously mediated ceasefires, but tensions often resurface.\n\nFor the Somali diaspora, particularly those originating from Sanaag, the instability hampers investment and family reunification. Many diaspora members send remittances to support relatives in these areas, and conflict disrupts livelihoods and access to basic services like healthcare and education. The Puntland government's call for peace may provide a window for external mediation and humanitarian access.\n\nAnalysts note that without sustained political engagement, ceasefires are fragile. The recent appeal by Puntland authorities is a step toward de-escalation, but long-term solutions require addressing root causes and involving local communities. International partners have also expressed readiness to support peace initiatives if both sides commit.\n\nRead the original report from Horseed Media here: https://horseedmedia.net/puntland-oo-war-ka-soo-saartay-dagaal-beeleed-ka-dhacay-gobolka-sanaag/424179/",
